---
import SupplierLayout from '@/layouts/SupplierLayout.astro';
import ProductGallery from '@/components/ProductGallery.astro';
import { BEDDING_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { supplierTitle } from '@/lib/seo';

const title = supplierTitle('Bedding');
const terms = [
  { label: 'Minimum order', value: '50 pieces per style. Mixed sizes in one order are quoted separately and shipped together.' },
  { label: 'Sampling', value: 'A sample goes out before bulk. We confirm the sample cost and the lead time on the quotation.' },
  { label: 'Lead time', value: 'Quoted per order, after the sample is approved. Sample run and bulk run are scheduled separately.' },
  { label: 'Sizes', value: 'Cut to US, UK or AU specs, or to your own sheet layout.' },
  { label: 'Payment', value: 'Terms come with the quotation and depend on order value and destination.' },
  { label: 'Documents', value: 'Commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading. Certificates where the project needs them.' },
];
---

<SupplierLayout
  title={title}
  description="Hotel bedding supplied wholesale from our Qingdao factory: flat sheets, duvet covers, pillowcases and mattress protectors. MOQ 50 pcs, 240 to 384 TC."
  h1="Hotel bedding supplier & wholesale manufacturer"
  breadcrumb="Hotel Bedding Supplier"
  serviceName="Hotel bedding supply and manufacturing"
  serviceType="Hotel bedding supply and manufacturing: flat sheets, duvet covers, pillowcases and mattress protectors"
  specs={BEDDING_SPECS}
  images={['hotel-bedding-supplier-hero', 'hotel-linens-hero']}
  specLead="All four yarn counts below are single-ply constructions. Nothing here is inflated by multi-ply counting."
  terms={terms}
  categoryHref="/hotel-linens/"
  categoryLabel="Hotel linens range"
>
  <p>
    Hotel bedding in our catalogue means flat sheets, duvet covers, pillowcases, mattress
    protectors, and the down-alternative comforters and pillows that go with them. We make all
    of it in Qingdao and ship wholesale. Trade accounts only, 50 pieces per style.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of what leaves the plant is custom hotel bedding: sizes cut from the property's own
    layout rather than picked off a stock list. That is normal for a hotel order, and it is
    why a catalogue with three standard sheet sizes will not get you far.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most inquiries we get are replacements, not new builds. A hotel is running low on a size, or
    the current supplier's thread count has quietly dropped and the linen no longer survives the
    laundry contract. Either way, the buyer arrives with a spec already in hand. The job is to
    match it, or to say clearly what changes if you move the price.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What a bedding spec actually contains</h2>
  <p>
    A hotel sheet order is more specific than a retail one, and the parts that matter are not
    the parts people talk about. The list below is what we need before we can quote a number
    that holds up:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>Mattress width and depth, per room type. A 20 cm and a 30 cm mattress take different flat sheet dimensions even at the same width.</li>
    <li>Yarn count, or the thread count you are on now.</li>
    <li>Construction: plain weave, sateen stripe, mercerized, or jacquard.</li>
    <li>Hem style and width, if the property has a housekeeping standard to match.</li>
    <li>Colour. White is standard. Anything else is a dye run with its own minimum.</li>
    <li>Quantity per size, and the month the first container has to arrive.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Skip any of these and the quote becomes a guess. We would rather ask twice than send a
    price for a sheet that turns out to be 10 cm short.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Thread count, without the marketing</h2>
  <p>
    Thread count is the number of warp and weft threads per square inch. Our price list gives
    the yarn numbers directly, so the count can be worked out rather than claimed:
    40s×40s at 145×95 works out to about 240, 60s×40s at 173×120 to about 293, and 60s×80s at
    200×(92+92) to about 384. The jacquard runs at 173×180, which counts to about 353.
  </p>
  <p>
    Numbers above 400 in hotel linen are usually reached by counting plies separately. We do not
    do that. A 240 TC single-ply percale and a "600 TC" two-ply sheet can be the same cloth.
    If a supplier's spec sheet reads 1,000 TC at a hotel price, ask what was counted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Higher count is not automatically better for a hotel. A finer yarn feels softer and wears
    faster under commercial laundry. In our experience on a 100-wash contract, 240 TC percale
    often outlives 384 TC sateen. That is a real trade-off and it belongs in the buying
    decision, not in a brochure.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Four constructions and where they fit</h2>
  <p>
    Percale is the plain weave. Crisp, matte, the classic hotel hand. It takes high-temperature
    laundry better than the other three, which is why it is the first spec we quote for
    four-star work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sateen stripe uses a cotton sateen weave with a woven band. It reads as more expensive on
    the bed and shows ironing marks less, but the sateen face is more prone to snagging in a
    busy laundry.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mercerized cotton goes through a caustic treatment that increases lustre and dye uptake.
    It looks brighter white and takes dye more evenly, which helps colour stay even across
    washes. Costs more per metre, and the difference is visible mainly under hotel lighting,
    not in a photo.
  </p>
  <p>
    Jacquard is woven on a dobby or jacquard loom, so the pattern is in the cloth rather than
    printed on it. The pattern does not wash off, though the colours still depend on the dye
    and the finishing. It does cost more to produce and the minimum run is higher.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Comforter or quilt</h2>
  <p>
    Our price list calls the down-alternative item a quilt; in the US the same construction is
    usually sold as a comforter. The two names describe the same build here, so quote it under
    whichever one your spec uses. If the spec means a different quilted construction, say so
    and we will match that instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also make a lighter summer quilt for properties that run warm rooms without air
    conditioning, and a down-alternative pillow in the same fill.
  </p>
  <ProductGallery
    images={[
      'bedding-comforter-hero',
      'bedding-comforter-detail',
      'bedding-pillow-stack',
      'bedding-pillow-detail',
    ]}
  />

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What happens after you send the spec</h2>
  <p>
    We come back with what we can hold to spec, what we would substitute, and the price gap
    between the two. Substitutions are usually yarn count, hem width or carton packing. We will
    flag any of those before production, not after.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then a sample. Check the hand feel, the seam and the finished size against your own
    measuring tape. If the sample is right, bulk runs. If it is not, better to find out on one
    sheet than on four thousand.
  </p>
</SupplierLayout>
